  <title>Shell integration and prompts</title>

<p>With <em>shell integration</em>, the shell tells <app>Pano Terminal</app>
  where each prompt and command starts and how the command ended. That is
  what <link xref="cmd-notifications#commands">command notifications</link>,
  jumping between prompts and copying the output of commands need.</p>

  <p><app>Pano Terminal</app> sets this up by itself for <app>bash</app>,
  <app>zsh</app> and <app>fish</app>, without changing your files, when it
  starts the shell of a profile. It does not for
  <link xref="pref-custom-command">custom commands</link>,
  <link xref="pref-login-shell">login shells</link> of bash, and terminals in
  containers. To turn it off:</p>
  <steps>
<item><p>Press the menu button in the top-right corner of the window and select <gui style="menuitem">Preferences</gui>.</p></item>
<item><p>In the <gui>Behavior</gui> group, turn off <gui>Shell Integration</gui>. This applies to new terminals.</p></item>
</steps>

  <section id="marks">
    <title>Command marks</title>
    <p>Marks next to the scrollbar show where the commands in the scrollback
    are. Marks of commands that failed are red.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="navigate">
    <title>Jump between prompts</title>
    <p>Press <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>Up</key></keyseq> to scroll to the prompt of the
    previous command, and <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>Down</key></keyseq> to scroll to the next
    one. Without shell integration, these keys keep their usual meaning in the
    terminal.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="copy-output">
    <title>Copy the output of a command</title>
    <p>Press <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>O</key></keyseq> to copy the output of the last
    command. To copy that of an earlier command, right-click its output and
    select <gui style="menuitem">Copy Output of This Command</gui>.</p>
  </section>

